.TH defrag.exfat 8
.SH NAME
defrag.exfat \- experimental journaled offline exFAT image defragmentation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B defrag/defrag.exfat
.B --dry-run
.I image
.PP
.B defrag/defrag.exfat
.I image
.B --journal
.I new-directory
.PP
.B defrag/defrag.exfat
.I image
.B --recover --journal
.I original-directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bottom line: use this experimental interface only with small, offline Linux
image files. Do not use it directly on user volumes. The normal build does not
install it.
.PP
The interface accepts regular single-volume image files up to 256 MiB. The
volume must have one FAT and no loop attachment. The support restrictions in
docs/defrag/README.md also apply. The interface rejects block devices, TexFAT,
unknown allocated entries, and bad-cluster layouts before file-system writes.
.PP
Analysis is read-only. Writes require a new private external journal containing
a full durable preimage, bound to the image inode and a transaction token.
After a failure or kill, retain the image and original journal and use
.BR --recover .
The interface does not guess when a journal is missing or corrupt.
.PP
The explicitly built C binary in build-defrag-tests is an internal unjournaled
worker. Invoking it directly bypasses the recovery protocol.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --dry-run, -a
Report allocation discontinuities, extents, feasibility and scope limits as JSON.
.TP
.B --journal directory
Specify a new private journal directory for an operation. For recovery, specify
the original directory.
.TP
.B --recover
Resolve an active transaction using verified undo data or a valid commit record.
.TP
.B --engine path
Select the internal worker; default build-defrag-tests/defrag.exfat.
.TP
.B --fsck path
Select the independent checker; default build-defrag-tests/fsck.exfat.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show all arguments. Fault and trace options are explicit qualification interfaces.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0: verified completion, analysis, no-op or recovery.
1: stopped without an active transaction.
2: invalid arguments.
3: recovery required; retain recovery data.
4: committed but cleanup/durability confirmation incomplete.
130/143: SIGINT/SIGTERM; the reported state is old after rollback, or new if
cancellation arrived during the commit batch.
.SH NOTES
Locks are advisory. Visible loop attachments are detected; exclusive access
across other mount namespaces or noncooperating writers cannot be guaranteed.
The storage model assumes truthful successful fsync and durable journal storage.
This is not a qualification for direct use on user volumes.
.PP
The additional tests/defrag/defrag_image.py workflow opens its source read-only,
verifies a private candidate independently, and publishes with an atomic
no-replace hard link. Failure after that link may leave visible valid output.
See docs/defrag/README.md and its ADRs for exact recovery and publication rules.
